import{_ as t,c as a,o as i,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.Vyheyqg9.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Reliable structured output","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"applied-ai/javascript/structured-output.md","filePath":"applied-ai/javascript/structured-output.md"}'),o={name:"applied-ai/javascript/structured-output.md"};function r(n,e,d,l,c,p){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="_3-reliable-structured-output" tabindex="-1">Reliable structured output <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-reliable-structured-output" aria-label="Permalink to “Reliable structured output”">​</a></h1><p>Free text works for conversation. Application logic often needs fields. A note classifier might return <code>{ category: &#39;ai&#39;, quote: &#39;API keys&#39; }</code> so the UI can display a category and supporting evidence.</p><p>“Respond in JSON” does not enforce your rules. Check three things: can the result be parsed, does it match the schema, and is the meaning supported?</p><h2 id="work-through-the-checks" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">1 </span>Work through the checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#work-through-the-checks" aria-label="Permalink to “1 Work through the checks”">​</a></h2><p>Run <code>npm run demo</code> in the <a href="./../../workbench/applied-ai/javascript/">starter</a>. The classifier validates a synthetic object against <code>Keep API keys on the server.</code> No model is called.</p><div class="language-js"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">js</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark" style="--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark:#e1e4e8;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;--shiki-dark-bg:#24292e;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">validateQuote</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">  { category: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&#39;ai&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, quote: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&#39;API keys&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> },</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">  &#39;Keep API keys on the server.&#39;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>validateDecision</code> accepts exactly <code>category</code> and <code>quote</code>, one of three labels and a bounded string. <code>validateQuote</code> requires a non-review decision to include an actual source quote. Change it to <code>browser credentials</code>: validation fails. Add an unexpected field: validation fails.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Candidate</th><th>Shape</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>{ category: &#39;ai&#39;, quote: &#39;API keys&#39; }</code></td><td>Valid</td><td>Exact substring</td><td>Eligible for semantic review</td></tr><tr><td><code>{ category: &#39;ai&#39;, quote: &#39;invented&#39; }</code></td><td>Valid</td><td>Missing</td><td>Reject</td></tr><tr><td><code>{ category: &#39;finance&#39;, quote: &#39;API keys&#39; }</code></td><td>Invalid label</td><td>Present</td><td>Reject</td></tr><tr><td><code>{ category: &#39;review&#39;, quote: &#39;&#39; }</code></td><td>Valid</td><td>Insufficient evidence allowed</td><td>Route to review</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A genuine quote still does not prove the category is right. A note can mention AI only to explain what it does not cover. Write category definitions and review borderline cases.</p><h2 id="define-a-useful-prompt" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">2 </span>Define a useful prompt <a class="header-anchor" href="#define-a-useful-prompt" aria-label="Permalink to “2 Define a useful prompt”">​</a></h2><p>Specify the task, label meanings, output fields and missing-evidence behavior. Separate source text from application instructions. Give a positive and ambiguous example if they clarify the boundary. Ask for checkable evidence or a short rationale; hidden reasoning is not an audit log.</p><p>For live work use your provider&#39;s supported structured-output mechanism, then validate locally. For Responses this is a <code>text.format</code> JSON-schema contract. Refusal and incomplete output still need handling. Start with a small schema; arbitrary validator features are not necessarily supported remotely.</p><h2 id="compare-with-a-baseline" tabindex="-1"><span class="hub-section-number">3 </span>Compare with a baseline <a class="header-anchor" href="#compare-with-a-baseline" aria-label="Permalink to “3 Compare with a baseline”">​</a></h2><p>Prepare ten synthetic notes: clear AI, clear other, ambiguous, empty, quoted instructions and misleading keywords. Write expected labels <strong>before</strong> changing prompts. Keep some cases aside for final confirmation. Record invalid-shape rate, unsupported-quote rate and actual category judgments separately. Passing JSON checks does not establish accuracy.</p><p><strong>Your exercise:</strong> implement the classifier with injected fake outputs first. Add a live adapter only if useful. Keep a failing counterexample and explain the correction without changing the expected answer to hide the error.</p><p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> treating <code>JSON.parse</code> as validation, accepting extra fields, trusting claimed confidence, and retrying invalid output without a limit. Do not “repair” a refusal into a positive classification.</p><p><strong>Done:</strong> invalid shapes and fabricated quotes fail, ambiguous cases reach review, and you can explain exact quote checking&#39;s limits. <a href="./tools-workflows.html">Next: tools</a>. Deeper material: <a href="./../../python-for-ai/model-sdk.html">schemas</a> and <a href="./../../python-for-ai/evaluation-lab.html">evaluation</a>.</p>`,17)])])}const g=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
